  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutheast Michigan will remain positioned between loosely organized \nsplit upper level flow thru the early week period, as a sprawling \n1028 mb high pressure system centered to the north maintains a high \ndegree of influence. This pattern effectively retains east to \nnortheast low level flow, ensuring a seasonably cool resident \nairmass holds firm during this time. 850 mb temperature projected to \ndip into the single digits, translating into highs both Monday and \nTuesday of lower 60s most locations. Mainly dry with breezy \nconditions as the profile remains dominated by larger scale \nsubsidence within a generally moisture starved profile. There \nremains a lower end rain chance near the Ohio border, as shortwave \nenergy rippling thru the southern stream into the Ohio valley may \nengage the deeper moisture still lingering near the Michigan-Ohio \nborder. Diurnal mixing will yield afternoon wind gusts generally in \nthe 20 to 25 knot range - slightly higher downwind of Saginaw Bay. \n\nWeak upper level system will drift across the Ohio valley throughout \nthe mid-week period. This system will attempt to pull moisture north \nand west toward the area Tuesday night into Wednesday. Broader \nascent offered by some degree of deformation may prove sufficient \ntoward generating some pockets of showers during this time, again \nmainly for areas south of the I-94 corridor. Otherwise, stretch of \ndry and stable conditions lasting through the end of the work week. \nLack of meaningful thermal advection as low level flow holds out of \nthe east-northeast maintains this stretch of below average \ntemperatures. Highs in the 60s with overnight lows dipping into the \nmid to upper 40s.  \n\nThe closed upper level low over the Ohio Valley is forecast to open \nup into a progressive trough and fill as it gets absorbed into the \nprimary northern stream westerlies by Friday. Simultaneously, the \nexpansive surface anticyclone centered over northern Ontario/Quebec \nwill slowly shift eastward toward the Canadian Maritimes. Building \nupper heights locally will solidify a gradually moderating thermal \nprofile while maintaining dry and stable background conditions. 850 \nmb temperatures will ease back toward the 10c range, allowing \nreadings to work back toward seasonal averages under nearly full \ninsolation. No rain expected thru the upcoming weekend, as \nconditions remains governed by low and mid level ridging.",
